      {
        "id": 176015,
        "name": "Belle Sorciere",
        "username": "Belle_Sorciere",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/belle_sorciere/{size}/1982_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-06-06T18:57:50.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Rabit\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/rabit/48/5774_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Rabit:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I think that's part of the charm of it, to me. They act like people, and like most people they do fairly stupid stuff. They don't understand how Manhatan views time, that they should/could change what he sees – and actually they couldn't, as he's already seen them do it and would see them taking the action to avoid what he's saying. That's part of the core of it all, that they have no choice in avoiding it.\"… timey-whimey…\"But yeah, Rorschach is certainly the most interesting of them, although he <em>is </em>psychotic.  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/confused.png?v=12\" title=\":confused:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":confused:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Actually, he really isn't. He doesn't show any signs of psychosis in the series.</p>\n<p>Did you actually mean something other than \"psychotic?\"</p>",
